  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 411 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThursday, the upper high sits over the Mid-Mississippi Valley with \none positively tilted ridge pushing off into the Atlantic and \nanother negatively tilted ridge forming to the northwest of the \nhigh, running through the Ozarks and Northern Plains, resulting in \nnorthwest flow into the Ohio Valley. Some model guidance has this \npushing low chances of precipitation into the CWA, but believe a \nbetter 10-30 percent chance of precipitation for some will arrive on \nFriday as a west to east oriented front is expected to drop south to \nour east as the axis of the upper ridge approaches the CWA. With any \nof these chances of precipitation, amounts and coverage is expected \nto remain low.  \n\nLooking into the weekend and beyond, consensus remains weak as the \ncore flow aloft remains north of the CWA. To start this period, \nsurface winds remain out of the south, bringing Gulf moisture and \nwarm temperatures northward, so any shortwave could produce \nconvection, but even without any rainfall, we can count on warm \ntemperatures to persist through at least the weekend as temperatures \nremain in the upper 80s to mid 90s.",
